Each of the three oscillator nodes charges its load capacitance \(C_L\) once per oscillation period \(T_0=1/f_0\). Thus, the average supply current is

\[
I_{DD,\text{avg}}
=3\frac{C_L V_{DD}}{T_0}
=3C_LV_{DD}f_0 .
\]

The equivalent resistance looking into the supply node is therefore

\[
R_{\text{in,DD}}
=\frac{V_{DD}}{I_{DD,\text{avg}}}
=\boxed{\frac{1}{3C_Lf_0}}.
\]

This is the effective resistance associated with the oscillator’s dynamic switching power; an ideal CMOS ring oscillator has no static DC supply current.

Using the simplified model, let the input voltage be \(v_1\). The amplifier output is modeled by a Thevenin source of voltage

\[
v_o'=-A_Vv_1
\]

in series with \(R_{D2}\). Thus, the current through the feedback path is

\[
i_F=\frac{v_1-v_o'}{R_F+R_{D2}}
=\frac{(1+A_V)v_1}{R_F+R_{D2}}.
\]

The total input current is

\[
i_{\text{in}}
=\frac{v_1}{R_{\text{in}}}+sC_{\text{in}}v_1+i_F.
\]

Therefore,

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\text{in}}(s)
=\frac{v_1}{i_{\text{in}}}
=
\left[
\frac{1}{R_{\text{in}}}
+sC_{\text{in}}
+\frac{1+A_V}{R_F+R_{D2}}
\right]^{-1}
}
\]

or equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\text{in}}
=
R_{\text{in}}
\parallel \frac{1}{sC_{\text{in}}}
\parallel
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}{1+A_V}
}.
\]

If \(C_{\text{in}}\) is neglected and \(R_{D2}\ll R_F\),

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{in,total}}
\approx
R_{\text{in}}\parallel \frac{R_F}{1+A_V}
}
\]

and for large \(A_V\), the feedback-controlled input resistance is approximately \(R_F/A_V\).
